/-!
# Flat ascent and etale descent for Koszul-regular immersions

Source: Stacks Project, Descent, Tag 0694, Koszul-regular clause; Divisors, Tag 067P
(blueprint: lem-sfb-koszul-immersion-flat-ascent-etale-descent).

Koszul regularity of an immersion means `IsKoszulRegularImmersionNear` at every
source point. This uses the existing finite Koszul-regular closed-chart predicate,
with varying lengths and with length zero allowed.
-/

@[expose] public section

set_option autoImplicit false

universe u v

open CategoryTheory CategoryTheory.Limits

namespace AlgebraicGeometry

/-- Koszul regularity of an immersion ascends under flat base change and is detected
by any jointly surjective set-indexed etale family on the target. No finiteness,
Noetherian, separation or codimension hypothesis is needed, and the index type may
be empty. The pullback projections are immersions by immersion base change.

Source: Stacks Project, Divisors, Tag 067P; Descent, Tag 0694, Koszul-regular clause
(blueprint: lem-sfb-koszul-immersion-flat-ascent-etale-descent).

Proof plan:
1. Factor `i` as a closed immersion into an open subscheme. Use the affine criterion
   `lem-sfb-koszul-closed-immersion-affine-criterion` near the image of a pullback
   point, and choose an affine neighbourhood inside any prescribed open in the
   pullback target. The affine relative-flatness criterion
   `lem-pc-affine-chart-relative-flat-criterion` makes its coordinate-ring map flat.
   By `lem-weil-pbc-quotient-base-change-closed-immersion-ideal`, the pulled-back
   ideal is the extended ideal. Its generators remain Koszul-regular by
   `lem-sc-koszul-regular-flat-base-change`. The affine criterion proves ascent.
2. Each etale arrow is flat, so ascent proves the forward descent implication.
3. For the reverse implication work on each affine open of the open target factor.
   Base change the etale covering and use its finite affine refinement
   `lem-weil-pbc-etale-covering-sieve-finite-affine-refinement`. Etale composition
   cancellation makes refinement arrows etale and hence flat. Open-immersion base
   change identifies the restrictions of the assumed regular charts. Apply ascent
   to obtain regular charts on each affine refinement member.
4. Apply `lem-sc-etale-standard-cover-cech`, with auxiliary abelian sheaf zero,
   to obtain an affine disjoint union and a faithfully flat coordinate-ring map.
   Quotient base change and the affine criterion identify its extended ideal as
   locally generated by finite Koszul-regular sequences on principal opens.
5. Apply `lem-sfb-koszul-ideal-faithfully-flat-descent`. Shrink its principal
   neighbourhoods into any prescribed open; localization is flat and preserves
   Koszul regularity. The affine criterion gives regularity of the original
   immersion at every source point. Length-zero sequences and empty sources are
   allowed throughout; the finite refinement is chosen on each affine separately. -/
theorem koszulRegularImmersion_flat_ascent_etale_descent
    {Z X : Scheme.{u}} (i : Z ⟶ X) [IsImmersion i] :
    (∀ (X' : Scheme.{u}) (q : X' ⟶ X), Flat q →
      (∀ z : Z, IsKoszulRegularImmersionNear i z) →
      ∀ z' : (pullback i q : Scheme.{u}), IsKoszulRegularImmersionNear (pullback.snd i q) z') ∧
    (∀ (A : Type v) (Xa : A → Scheme.{u}) (q : ∀ a : A, Xa a ⟶ X),
      (∀ a : A, Etale (q a)) →
      (∀ x : X, ∃ (a : A) (y : Xa a), q a y = x) →
      ((∀ z : Z, IsKoszulRegularImmersionNear i z) ↔
        ∀ (a : A) (z' : (pullback i (q a) : Scheme.{u})),
          IsKoszulRegularImmersionNear (pullback.snd i (q a)) z')) := by
  sorry

end AlgebraicGeometry
